Chapter 14: Tree algorithms

A tree is a connected, acyclic graph that consists of n nodes and n-1 edges. 
Removing any edge from a tree divides it into two components.
Add any edge to a tree create a cycle.
There is always a unique path between 2 nodes of a tree.

acyclic : Khong chu trinh

The leaves of a tree are the nodes with degree 1. Only 1 neighbor.
A rooted tree, one of the nodes is appointed the root of the tree, all other nodes are placed underneath the root.

In a rooted tree:
+ children of a node is its lower neighbor
+ parent of a node is its upper neighbor. Each node has only one parent except the root.

Structure of a node is recursive: each node of the tree acts as the root of a subtree that contains the node itself and all nodes
that are in the subtrees of its children.

Tree traversal

General graph traversal algorithms can be used to traverse the nodes of a tree.
Traversal of a tree is easier to implement than that of general graph, because there are no cycles in the tree and it is
not possible to reach a node from multiple directions.

A typical way to traverse a tree is to start a depth-first search at an arbitrary node.
use DFS to traverse a tree

void dfs(int s, int e) {
    // process node s
    for (auto u : adj[s]) {
        if (u != e) dfs(u, s);
    }
}

s : start or source
e : excluded or parent

The purpose of the parameter e is to make sure that the search only moves to nodes that have not been visited yet.
function call starts the search at node x:
dfs(x, 0);

in the first call e = 0 -> because there is no previous node.

void dfs(int s, int e) {
    count[s] = 1;
    for(auto u : adj[s]) {
        if(u == e) continue;
        dfs(u, s);
        count[s] += count[u];
    }
}